description:
Parse the head of an HTTP request and decide how its body is delimited (lesson 3.02):

```cpp
struct Head
{
    std::string method, target, version;
    std::vector<std::pair<std::string, std::string>> headers;   // names in lower case, in order
};

enum class BodyKind { None, Length, Chunked, Invalid };
struct Body { BodyKind kind; std::size_t length; };

std::optional<Head> parseHead(const std::string& head);
std::vector<std::string> headerValues(const Head& h, const std::string& name);
Body requestBody(const Head& h);
```

- **parseHead** takes everything before the blank line. Lines are separated by `\r\n`. The request line is exactly three parts separated by single spaces, with a version starting `HTTP/`. Each header line has a name (non-empty, no spaces or tabs) before its first `:`, and a value with spaces and tabs trimmed from both ends; names are stored in lower case. Any malformed line makes the whole head invalid.
- **headerValues** returns every value of a header (compare names ignoring case), in order.
- **requestBody**:
  - `Transfer-Encoding` and `Content-Length` both present, or several `Content-Length` values that are not all identical: `Invalid`.
  - `Transfer-Encoding` present: `Chunked` if its value is exactly `chunked` (ignoring case), otherwise `Invalid`.
  - `Content-Length` present: it must be 1 to 15 digits only, else `Invalid`; then `Length` with that value (0 is allowed).
  - Neither: `None`.

